Apple has recently released their  brand-new 20/20 MacBook Air so we've now  got apples complete lineup of mac books  starting with the air for $1000 the base  13-inch pro for $1300 the higher-end  13-inch Pro for $1,800 and the 16 inch  MacBook Pro for $2,400 and to make it  even better  each of these MacBook Pros is currently  on sale on Amazon and we'll have links  of them down below in the description  now let's get start with comparing  literally everything we can between all  of these Mac books starting with  portability in terms of weight the new  20/20 air is now 2.8 pounds still a bit  lighter than the 13-inch MacBook Pro and  much lighter than the 16 inch from above  the air is basically just as large as  the 13-inch pro so portability should be  pretty similar and the 16 inch pro is of  course much larger than the rest the  design of the air hasn't changed since  last year so it still has a wedge shape  compared to a more flat slate shape on  the MacBook Pros each of these Mac books  has a high quality retina resolution  display but the airs display isn't as  nice it's quite a bit dimmer than the  pros that each have an incredible 500  nits of brightness and it's definitely  not as color accurate since every  MacBook Pro has P 3 wide color gamut  which has about 25% more colors than  100% srgb which is a must-have for photo  editors as far as display sharpness they  all have around the same Retina quality  227 pixels per inch and every macbook in  the lineup comes with true toe  technology which adjusts the white  balance of the display to match the  environment around you the 16 inch pro  has the biggest benefit of having  smaller bezels compared to the rest of  the Mac books so it looks quite a bit  nicer speaker quality and volume is a  huge deal for some people especially for  me and we've known that the 16 inch  MacBook Pro has by far the best speakers  out of the lineup one interesting point  is that the speakers on the MacBook Air  are actually slightly worse than the  ones on the 2019 model but they're still  better than most Windows laptops  [Music]  [Applause]  [Music]  [Music]  now onto the keyboards the 16-inch Pro  and the new 2020 MacBook Air pack  Apple's latest magic keyboard compared  to the butterfly keyboard on the 13-inch  MacBook Pro models there is seriously a  light and a difference between the two  you get so much more key travel and it's  a much better typing experience on the  new magic keyboard there are rumors that  the 13-inch Pro could be coming this  summer with the new keyboard but not  everyone can wait that long to buy apart  from that the MacBook Airs keyboard  slopes downward towards the front edge  so it's actually a bit more comfortable  to type on compared to the pros every  single MacBook in the lineup comes with  the touch ID sensor built into the power  button which makes it really convenient  to log in use Apple pay online and  install apps off the web without having  to enter your password however every  macbook pro comes with the touch bar an  OLED display that replaces the top row  of keys and is fully customizable but  not that many people like it because  it's more difficult to do things like  adjust display brightness and volume the  benefit of the 16 inch pro is that it  actually gets a physical Escape key so  it's much more convenient for  programmers or other people who use that  key a lot thankfully the MacBook Air  still has physical keys so that's a  great option as well since it now comes  with a quad-core processor now let's  talk about the port's every port on  every MacBook supports Thunderbolt 3 but  both the 13-inch air in the lower-end  MacBook Pro come with only two of these  ports compared to four on the more  expensive pros if you're a basic user  that doesn't connect to external  displays or connect external devices  very often you'll probably be fine with  one of the two port models and you also  have the choice of buying a Thunderbolt  3 hub just in case you do need more  ports now if you love large trackpads  the 16 inch pro has the biggest one of  them all followed by the 13 inch pro and  then the air that has a slightly smaller  trackpad for battery life the 2020  MacBook Air actually got rated down to  11 hours instead of last year's 12 which  now matches the battery life of the 16  inch pro and beats the 10 hours on the  13-inch pro models getting into  performance the 20/20 MacBook Air now  has a $100 quad-core option which we  absolutely recommend  for everyone interested in the air so  we'll be using the $1,100 air model for  our testing the 13-inch pros get even  faster quad-core CPUs and the 16 inch  pro gets a 6 core that can actually be  upgraded to an 8 core if you're willing  to spend another $300 first willsez  single core performance in Geekbench 5  for tasks that use just one core  surprisingly the new 20/20 air has the  highest single core score even higher  than on the 16 inch pro this is probably  because it's the first MacBook with an  Intel 10th generation processor so that  shows it's great for simple tasks  looking at multi-core performance we can  see that the MacBook Air is finally  catching up to the 13-inch MacBook Pro  with the pro still having a 22% higher  score and the 16 inch pro gives you a  big jump in performance as well we  decided to look at performance per  dollar using the full retail price of  each MacBook and the new MacBook Air is  now right behind the pro with a decent  amount of raw power for the cache one  thing you won't find on the spec sheet  is that the 20/20 MacBook Air has only  one fan just like on the $1300 MacBook  Pro however the one on the air isn't  actually connected to the CPUs heatsink  so it's not very effective at  dissipating heat the more expensive  MacBook Pros have duel fans so they  should perform better for longer  workloads to test this we ran Cinebench  R 20 a more realistic benchmark which is  long enough to heat the systems up and  see how well the cooling systems work  surprisingly the single fan on the  $1,300 pro didn't really limit  performance like we thought it would but  the 20/20 MacBook Air performed way  worse than expected because of the fan  not being connected to the heatsink and  the 16 inch MacBook Pro of course  performed by far the best in terms of  performance for the cache in long term  heavy workloads like instead of inch are  20 the air is actually now the loser  because it can't keep itself cool we  then tested graphics performance using  Geekbench fives mettle test and the 16  inch MacBook Pro destroyed the rest of  the Mac books and we are surprised to  see how well the graphics on the 20/20  air performed again because of the new  graphics ship in the 10 gen Intel CPUs  also the 13  MacBook Pro scores went down since last  year which was really weird possibly due  to a new metal testing method in the  latest Geekbench 5 update and you can  see this when looking at graphics  performance per dollar the 6-inch Pro is  now the winner in terms of value for  graphics power but the new 20/20 air is  not too far behind and since the 16 inch  Pro has such a good graphics chip we  decided to test gaming performance using  Unigine 7 Xtreme preset and it  completely obliterated the rest of the  lineup with more than four times the  gaming performance of the $1,800.00 Pro  so if you want to do some gaming on the  side by the 16 inch Pro we also tested  the SSD speeds and here the $1300 Pro is  the loser because it's the only one that  comes with only 128 gigs of storage you  actually have to pay $1500 to get a  model with 256 gigs and for that reason  the air is actually quite a bit faster  in terms of writing speed even faster  than on the $1,800 13-inch Pro but the  16 inch pro wins because of its base 512  gigabyte SSD and in terms of read speeds  the $1,800.00 pro is quite a lot faster  with the 16 inch pro being the clear  winner so with all those performance  tests out of the way let's break down  which macbook you should buy for your  specific use case the 20/20 MacBook Air  with the quad-core CPU for $1100 is now  the best choice unless you're gonna use  it for a heavy tasks like video or photo  editing all of the time if you just do a  bit of productivity work the air might  not perform well for long term tasks but  it'll still get the job done and is  actually very snappy for light tasks the  major benefit is of course the lower  $1,100 price tag  while having double the storage as a  $1300 pro and the new updated magic  keyboard which is much more reliable  better yet we experienced about 20% more  battery life on the air compared to the  13-inch Pro now if you actually do a lot  of productivity work like video editing  the 13-inch pro is a great choice  because the cooling system is a lot  better and not only that but it packs a  brighter and more color accurate display  for things like photo editing the only  downside is that you have to  with the butterfly keyboard but of  course you can hold off until this  summer when we may potentially see the  release of a 13-inch MacBook Pro with  the magic keyboard now that $1,800  13-inch Pro is in a really tough spot  because it honestly isn't that much more  powerful than the $1300 Pro it just has  a few luxury improvements like an extra  two Thunderbolt three ports faster SSD  speeds the extra cooling fan and better  speakers I would honestly recommend  everyone to completely ignore the $1,800  Pro and buy the 16 inch pro instead  because of the newer design new features  like the magic keyboard the 512 gigabyte  base SSD and much more performance this  is definitely the MacBook Pro that I  would recommend people to buy if they  have the budget to spend on a MacBook  it's just better in so many ways and  it's currently on sale for 2287 on  Amazon so use the link below if you're  interested so there you have it if  you're still confused on which one to  buy comment down below and we'll help  you out and if you want to see more  videos on the new 20/20 MacBook Air  check out one of those two videos right  over there and click the circle a box to  subscribe thanks for watching and we'll  see you in the next video  [Music]

